## Proof

We interpret $p \in \mathbb{Q}[X]$ as a polynomial in two indeterminates (since it is evaluated at the pair $(a_{n+1}, a_n)$), i.e., $p \in \mathbb{Q}[X, Y]$. The argument is identical for any finite number of indeterminates.

**Upper bound.** A sequence $(a_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ satisfying $a_{n+2} = p(a_{n+1}, a_n)$ for all $n$ is completely determined by the choice of:
- the polynomial $p \in \mathbb{Q}[X, Y]$, and
- the initial values $(a_0, a_1) \in \mathbb{Q}^2$.

Indeed, once $p$, $a_0$, and $a_1$ are fixed, the recurrence uniquely determines $a_2 = p(a_1, a_0)$, then $a_3 = p(a_2, a_1)$, and so on by induction.

Now, $\mathbb{Q}[X, Y]$ is countable: a polynomial in two variables is a finite sum $\sum c_{ij} X^i Y^j$ with $c_{ij} \in \mathbb{Q}$, and the set of such finite subsets of the countable set $\{X^i Y^j : i, j \geq 0\}$ with rational coefficients is countable (it is a countable union of countable sets, or equivalently a countable set of finite sequences of rationals).

Also, $\mathbb{Q}^2$ is countable.

Therefore, the set of all triples $(p, a_0, a_1) \in \mathbb{Q}[X,Y] \times \mathbb{Q}^2$ is countable (product of two countable sets). Since every valid sequence arises from at least one such triple, the set of all such sequences has cardinality **at most** $\aleph_0$.

**Lower bound.** Consider the polynomial $p(X, Y) = 0 \in \mathbb{Q}[X, Y]$. For any $(a_0, a_1) \in \mathbb{Q}^2$, the sequence
$$a_0,\; a_1,\; 0,\; 0,\; 0,\; \ldots$$
satisfies $a_{n+2} = 0 = p(a_{n+1}, a_n)$ for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$. Distinct pairs $(a_0, a_1)$ yield distinct sequences (they differ in at least one of the first two terms). Since $|\mathbb{Q}^2| = \aleph_0$, there are **at least** $\aleph_0$ such sequences.

**Conclusion.** The set of all such sequences has cardinality at most $\aleph_0$ and at least $\aleph_0$, so its cardinality is exactly $\aleph_0$.

$$\boxed{\aleph_0}$$

